# ButtonGroup — buttons joined into one control: a segmented switch, a toolbar, a
# save-with-a-cancel-beside-it. The point is that they read as one thing with
# several settings rather than as several unrelated presses.
#
#     use ButtonGroup():
#         button "Day" -> setRange("day")
#         button "Week" -> setRange("week")
#         button "Month" -> setRange("month")
#
# The buttons are the CALLER'S, written into the slot, because a button's whole
# content is its label and its action and both belong to the call site — there is
# no varargs and no way to forward a call site's arguments through an `action`
# reference, which is the same wall `forms/form.fct` records when it explains why
# `SubmitButton` no longer exists.
#
# ── THE SEPARATORS ARE GAPS, NOT BORDERS, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE ──────────────
#
# The obvious rule is `button + button { border-left: 1px solid }`, and it is
# fragile here for the reason this library keeps writing down: a `use` or an `if`
# renders an element around what it produces, so two buttons written through
# either one are not DOM siblings and the adjacent-sibling selector matches
# nothing. So the group's own BACKGROUND is the separator: `gap: 1px` over a line
# colour, with each button painting itself opaque. Nothing depends on sibling
# order, and a group whose middle button is inside an `if` still looks like one
# control.
#
# The `if` wrapper is flattened anyway (`display: contents`), so a conditional
# button is a real flex item and gets its own separator. That rule is narrow on
# purpose — direct children of the group, no class attribute, which is exactly
# what a runtime wrapper looks like and exactly what a caller's own classed box
# does not.
#
# The rounded ends come from the group clipping its children (`overflow: hidden`
# plus its own radius), so no `:first-child` / `:last-child` rule is needed and
# the group is round at the ends however its buttons were produced.
app FacetUI:
    css:
        [data-fa-mount] .x-btngroup { display: inline-flex; flex-direction: row;
                     align-items: stretch; flex-wrap: nowrap; gap: 1px; padding: 0;
                     border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden;
                     border: 1px solid var(--x-line, #2f3336);
                     background: var(--x-line, #2f3336); }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-btngroup button { border: none; border-radius: 0; margin: 0;
                     align-self: stretch; padding: 8px 14px; font-size: 14px;
                     font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.3; white-space: nowrap; cursor: pointer;
                     color: var(--fa-fg); background: var(--fa-bg); }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-btngroup button:hover { color: var(--fa-fg);
                     background: var(--x-hover, rgba(239,243,244,.06)); }
        # A runtime wrapper — an `if` or a region-carrying `use` — renders a div
        # with no class. Flattening it makes the button inside a real item of this
        # flex row, so it gets a separator like every other one.
        [data-fa-mount] .x-btngroup > div:not([class]),
        [data-fa-mount] .x-btngroup > .fa-use { display: contents; }
        # The stretched variant, for a group that should fill the width it is in
        # (a segmented control across a card, a two-button bar under a form).
        [data-fa-mount] .x-btngroup-fill { display: flex; width: 100%; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-btngroup-fill button { flex: 1 1 0; }
    component ButtonGroup():
        row class "x-btngroup":
            slot
    component ButtonGroupFill():
        row class "x-btngroup x-btngroup-fill":
            slot
